---
description: Secrets only in function secrets, env files never tracked, CORS allow-list, request limits, PDF content as data
alwaysApply: true
---

# Security and secrets

- The model API key lives only in the Edge Function's secrets (set by `deploy-api.yml` from the
  repository's GitHub secrets) and, for local runs, in the gitignored `infra/dev/functions.env`.
  The browser carries no key of any kind; the function runs with `verify_jwt = false`.
- `.env` and `.env.*` files are never tracked; only templates with placeholders are committed
  (`.env.example`, `infra/dev/functions.env.example`). Never print, log or paste a key value.
- `bun run hygiene` (part of `bun run check` and CI) fails on secret-looking tokens, tracked env
  files and absolute home paths, and reports only `file:line`, never the matched text.
- CORS is an exact-match allow-list (`ALLOWED_ORIGINS`): a foreign or missing `Origin` gets 403
  with no CORS headers, before any other work is done.
- Limits: the body is capped (`MAX_BODY_BYTES`, 413), requests per client IP are rate limited
  in Postgres (`RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE`, 429 with `Retry-After`), and the request schema caps
  page text and the number and size of page images. The browser rejects non-PDF and oversized
  files before extracting any text.
- PDF content is data, never instructions. It reaches the model inside `<document>` tags, and
  the system prompt tells the model to ignore any instruction found there.
- Model output is untrusted: it is validated against the strict schema and rendered as text.
  `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` is banned by lint.
- Before upload, the page tells the user that the document text (and images of pages without a
  text layer) is sent to an AI API (`upload.privacyNotice`). Keep that notice visible.
